WEBVTT

NOTE placeholder captions for V1 from the storyboard narration (temp)

V1-1-1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.763
A team has a slow, painful process.

V1-1-2
00:00:02.763 --> 00:00:09.079
A faster technology arrives, and they put it on top of the process as it is.

V1-1-3
00:00:09.079 --> 00:00:11.053
The problem doesn't go away.

V1-1-4
00:00:11.053 --> 00:00:15.000
It gets locked in, faster and at a larger scale.

V1-2-1
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.750
In 1990, Harvard Business Review published an article called Reengineering Work: Don't Automate, Obliterate.

V1-2-2
00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.393
Obliterate means wipe out.

V1-2-3
00:00:22.393 --> 00:00:32.661
The principle: if you run new technology on a broken process without taking it apart first, you only speed up the inefficiency you already have.

V1-2-4
00:00:32.661 --> 00:00:35.125
It was written about ordinary computing.

V1-2-5
00:00:35.125 --> 00:00:38.000
It applies to AI just as well.

V1-3-1
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.800
Case one.

V1-3-2
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:43.600
The early 1980s, the accounts payable department of a US car company.

V1-3-3
00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:45.200
500 people work there.

V1-3-4
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:49.200
A Japanese company runs the same department with about 100.

V1-3-5
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:50.400
Five times fewer.

V1-3-6
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.600
The US company wants to close that gap.

V1-3-7
00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.000
Before you see what they found, make the first call yourself.

V1-4-1
00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.930
Mapping revealed the real reason.

V1-4-2
00:00:59.930 --> 00:01:04.175
Most of the work went into manually reconciling three separate documents.

V1-4-3
00:01:04.175 --> 00:01:08.807
The gap was not closed by faster systems for the existing process.

V1-4-4
00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:16.140
Instead, the process itself was rewritten: payment is now triggered directly from the receipt record, with no separate invoice.

V1-4-5
00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:20.000
Per reports, the headcount needed fell by about 75 percent.

V1-5-1
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:20.800
Case two.

V1-5-2
00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.400
A Swedish fintech, per public reports, 2024 to 2025.

V1-5-3
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.600
An AI agent starts handling its customer conversations: about 2.3 million a month.

V1-5-4
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:36.000
Per reports, the drop in service load is on the scale of about 700 service reps.

V1-5-5
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Handling time falls from about 11 minutes to under two.

V1-6-1
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.656
These figures come from company publications and media reports.

V1-6-2
00:01:43.656 --> 00:01:48.531
They're here for learning, not as proof that people should be replaced.

V1-6-3
00:01:48.531 --> 00:01:53.000
Then, by early 2025, the company starts rehiring human service reps.

V1-6-4
00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:57.469
On simple requests, the AI performed about as well as people.

V1-6-5
00:01:57.469 --> 00:02:02.344
On complex ones, like disputes or personally difficult cases, quality dropped noticeably.

V1-6-6
00:02:02.344 --> 00:02:03.969
So what went wrong?

V1-6-7
00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:06.000
Decide before you hear it.

V1-7-1
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.552
Management admitted that focusing too much on efficiency hurt service quality.

V1-7-2
00:02:10.552 --> 00:02:18.000
The fix was a hybrid model: AI for the routine, a person when empathy and judgment are needed.

V1-8-1
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.143
Two cases, decades apart, with completely different technology.

V1-8-2
00:02:21.143 --> 00:02:22.321
The same lesson.

V1-8-3
00:02:22.321 --> 00:02:25.071
Process mapping is not a bureaucratic step.

V1-8-4
00:02:25.071 --> 00:02:32.143
It's what separates a change that works from a change that has to be fixed after the fact.

V1-8-5
00:02:32.143 --> 00:02:35.286
And notice: both stories are told in numbers.

V1-8-6
00:02:35.286 --> 00:02:36.857
Headcount, handling time, quality.

V1-8-7
00:02:36.857 --> 00:02:40.000
Without a number, nobody can check a change.
